60 calls; the provider is down for the middle third.

                              seconds    ok  failed  shed  upstream calls
  no protection                  8.5s    40      20     0              60
  retry only                    41.4s    40      20     0             120
  retry + circuit breaker        6.2s    20       3    37              32

Read the last column first. A retry loop turned 60 calls into 120,
and every one of those extra calls landed on a provider that was already
failing. That is the retry stampede: the moment a dependency wobbles, its
load goes up rather than down.

The breaker cut it to 32 and the window from 41s to 6s, by giving up
quickly instead of slowly — 37 calls were shed without being tried at all.

Now read the cost, because there is one. Successes fell from 40 to 20: the
breaker sheds every call while it is open, and some of those would have
succeeded — the provider came back before the cooldown expired and the
breaker did not know yet.

That is the trade, and it is not optional. A breaker converts slow failures
into fast ones and pays for it with false negatives during recovery. Tune the
cooldown too long and you are down after the provider is up; too short and
you are back to the stampede.

--- and the failure mode a breaker does not catch ---

  twenty calls over six workers: twelve to a slow dependency, eight to a
  fast endpoint that has nothing to do with it

                               window  slow ok  shed   fast p95
  every worker available         0.8s       12     0       846ms
  bulkhead of 2                  0.4s        2    10        31ms

Nothing errored in either run, so no breaker would have tripped. The
dependency is not broken — it has become slow, which is more common and more
dangerous, because a slow dependency quietly consumes every worker in the
process and takes down the endpoints that do not use it at all.

Read the last column. The fast endpoint's p95 went from 846ms to 31ms —
28x — and it has nothing to do with the slow dependency. Without the bulkhead it
spent its life queued behind twelve calls it never made.

The bulkhead paid for that by shedding 10 calls to the slow dependency. That is a
real cost and the entire point: you have decided, in advance, which work is
allowed to consume the process. Decide it in advance or the slowest dependency
decides it for you.
